<reference_puzzle index="1" source="train" puzzle_id="538b439f" lines="122-142">
<description>
Reflect the solid rectangular source objects across the full separator line and color the gap with the separator color.

Input structure:
- A dominant background contains one full-width or full-height one-pixel
  separator line in a non-background color.
- One other foreground color forms solid rectangular objects, all strictly on the same side of that separator.
- Remaining non-background pixels are scattered distractors.

Rule:
- Keep the input grid shape and unchanged pixels outside each affected corridor.
- Mirror each source object across the separator line.
- For every source object, recolor the rectangular strip between the
  original object and its reflection to the separator color, then paint the
  reflected object in the source color.

Key insight:
- The full straight line is a mirror axis; the real objects are identified
  structurally as the only solid rectangles confined to one side, while
  scattered pixels are noise that may be overwritten inside the corridor.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="1190bc91" lines="36-49">
<description>
Complete a sparse grid by casting occluding diagonal rays from the longest run and filling the remaining diagonal-connected regions from the short bars.

Input structure
- Black-background grid with one unique longest 4-connected foreground object.
- The longest object is a horizontal or vertical multicolor run; all other foreground objects are shorter monochrome bars.

Rule
- On a blank canvas, process the longest run from left to right or top to bottom; each pixel emits four 45-degree rays that stop at the first already-painted pixel.
- Each remaining monochrome bar fills every still-blank pixel reachable from it by diagonal steps.

Key insight
- Earlier pixels in the long run occlude later diagonal rays, and those ray barriers partition the background into diagonal-connected regions for the short bars.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="689c358e" lines="72-88">
<description>
Mark each arrow-like sprite's pointed frame endpoint with its color and the opposite endpoint black.

Input structure:
- The grid has a one-pixel solid rectangular frame around a dominant interior background.
- Inside the frame are monochrome six-pixel sprites shaped as asymmetric T arrows.
- Each sprite has a length-4 bar and a length-3 crossbar one cell closer to the pointed end.

Rule:
- For each sprite, follow its bar axis from the shorter end to the frame.
- Paint that frame endpoint with the sprite color.
- Paint the opposite endpoint on the same row or column black.
- Leave the interior background, sprites, and all other frame pixels unchanged.

Key insight:
- The direction is encoded by the crossbar's off-center position: the nearer end is the pointing end.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="eval" puzzle_id="f560132c" lines="259-273">
<description>
Assemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the "pieces"), scattered across the grid.
- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.

Rule:
- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.
- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.
- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.

Key insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="98c475bf" lines="62-76">
<description>
Erase the complete interior glyph and expand each endpoint marker pair into its color's full glyph.

Input structure
- The grid is 20x20 with background inside two same-color full-height vertical frame bars.
- Inside the frame there is one complete color glyph and one or more other colors appearing only as two endpoint markers in the same row.
- Marker endpoints are always in the first and last interior columns.

Rule
- Keep the vertical frame unchanged.
- Erase any complete interior glyph.
- For each endpoint marker pair, use that marker color's learned glyph template and anchor its horizontal bar on the marker row.

Key insight
- Color identifies which glyph shape to draw; the two endpoint pixels are not copied objects, they are anchors for reconstructing that color's full symbol.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="8b7bacbf" lines="42-56">
<description>
Fill enclosed regions along a line seeded by unique border pixels.

Input structure:
- bg = most common color.
- Colored lines/drawings on the grid.
- Unique single-pixel colors on the grid border mark seed endpoints of each line.

Rule:
- Each border seed pixel connects (8-connected) to a line drawing.
- Find closed bg cavities whose boundary touches that line.
- Flood-fill each such cavity with the seed pixel's color.

Key insight: Only cavities reachable through the seed's own line get filled; the seed's unique color acts as a per-line fill label.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="7" source="eval" puzzle_id="abc82100" lines="70-86">
<description>
Use local source-target-pattern examples to replace matching source pixels.

Input structure:
- Black background with isolated colored pixels and teal (8) pattern components.
- A rule example is a 4-neighbor colored pair source->target whose continued
  direction reaches an anchor touching a teal pattern component.

Rule:
- For each rule example, copy that teal component onto every other pixel of
  source.color, recoloring the copied component to target.color.
- Translation maps the rule anchor onto the source pixel being replaced.
- Remove teal components and all pixels used by rule examples.

Key insight: The two adjacent colored pixels define the color substitution and
the anchor point; the nearby teal component is the stencil that gets recolored.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="8" source="train" puzzle_id="a6953f00" lines="45-63">
<description>
Extract the top-left or top-right 2x2 corner crop selected by the top edge clue.

Input structure
- The input is a square color grid whose answer is one of the two 2x2 crops
  touching the top edge and a grid corner.
- A repeated color on the top row, when present, acts as the primary side cue.
- Otherwise, the two top corner colors have different whole-grid frequencies.

Rule
- If a top-row color repeats, find the repeated top-row occurrence nearest to
  a left or right edge and take the 2x2 top-corner crop on that side.
- If the top row has no repeated color, compare the whole-grid frequencies of
  the two top-corner colors and take the side whose corner color is rarer.

Key insight
- The task is not asking for an arbitrary 2x2 search: the decisive information
  is on the top edge, and it selects which top corner crop to keep.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="9" source="train" puzzle_id="7e4d4f7c" lines="9-24">
<description>
Compress the L-shaped cue to three rows and add a pink copy of the top-row foreground mask.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background color, a two-color top row, and all
foreground pixels below the top row lie in the first column as vertical markers.

Rule
Keep the input top row as output row 0 and the first marker row as output row 1.
Output row 2 is background except at the top-row foreground columns, which are
recolored pink.

Key insight
The lower repeated marker rows only identify the vertical cue; the generated
row is a recolored mask of the horizontal cue in the top row.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="10" source="train" puzzle_id="99caaf76" lines="56-75">
<description>
Move each marked cross object away from its marker to the grid edge while rotating the colored cross 180 degrees.

Input structure:
- The background is the dominant color.
- Each 8-connected foreground object has nine pixels: a 4-pixel marker-color
  sprite attached to a 5-pixel non-marker colored cross.
- Each object bbox is either 3x6 or 6x3, so the marker sprite is on exactly
  one side of the cross.

Rule:
- Move the whole object straight away from its marker side until the object
  bbox touches the corresponding grid edge.
- Keep the marker sprite unchanged within the object.
- Rotate the 3x3 colored cross block by 180 degrees before placing it.

Key insight:
The marker sprite is a directional tail; the cross is the leading payload,
and only that payload turns around during the slide to the edge.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="11" source="train" puzzle_id="6ffe8f07" lines="49-64">
<description>
Cast yellow horizontal and vertical rays from the teal rectangle until blue blockers stop them.

Input structure:
- A black-background grid containing one solid teal rectangle.
- Blue objects are blockers; other non-background objects may lie in the ray paths.
- Yellow is absent from the input and is the output paint color.

Rule:
- From every row of the teal rectangle, cast rays left and right to the grid edge.
- From every column of the teal rectangle, cast rays up and down to the grid edge.
- A ray stops before a blue pixel; all crossed non-blue, non-teal pixels become yellow.

Key insight:
The teal rectangle defines a full cross-shaped projection, while blue objects are walls and red/background cells are simply paintable space.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="12" source="train" puzzle_id="edcc2ff0" lines="47-63">
<description>
Keep only the lower-panel colors named by the top markers and expand each marker into an object-count bar.

Input structure
A black instruction strip sits above a non-black rectangular scene panel.  The strip contains one-cell markers
in the first column, and each marker color is a candidate object color in the lower panel.  The lower panel has
a unique dominant background color and scattered 4-connected foreground objects.

Rule
In the lower panel, preserve objects whose colors appear as top markers and recolor every other foreground
object to the panel background.  In the instruction strip, replace each marker by a horizontal run from column 0
whose length is the number of 4-connected lower-panel objects of that marker color; erase markers with count 0.

Key insight
The top strip is a color filter plus a compact count summary: marker colors select which lower-panel objects
survive, and their bars report the number of surviving objects by color.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

